# The build context is the repository root, and the owner's disk holds the
# retired runtime (ADR-037: unpublished, not deleted) plus the virtualenvs
# and caches of every tree that ever had a package. None of it belongs in a
# container that serves the canonical data layer — and the context is uploaded
# before the Dockerfile is even read, so a large context is a slow build on
# every source change.

.git/
.github/

**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/*.pyc
**/*.log

# The retired runtime, still on disk.
sys_ikigai/
src/mesh/
interfaces/
vibe-ops/
archive/
src/agents/
src/tuiboard/
src/solverforge_calendar/
src/ikigai/src/agents/
src/ikigai/src/chat/
src/ikigai/src/mcp_server/
src/ikigai/src/plugins/
src/ikigai/src/observability/
src/ikigai/bin/

# The root-level tooling of the retired system.
tests/
scripts/
bin/

# The tests run in CI, not in the container.
src/ikigai/tests/

# `docs/` is not served by the api or the bot, and the Dockerfile does not
# copy it — so it only makes the build context slower. `vault/`, `specs/` and
# `code-docs/` ARE copied — the sync service reads the vault prose.
docs/

# `strategics/` is published — the PT-BR prose is part of the branch, and the
# image copies it. The vendored `planning-with-files/` fork beside it is not:
# it is a third-party repository with its own `.git/`, thousands of files the
# image has no use for, and the context is uploaded before the Dockerfile is
# even read. Excluding it keeps the image identical on the owner's machine and
# on a fresh clone.
strategics/planning-with-files
